WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.440
From death by duffel bag to drugged up cubs,
Cocaine Bear has all the madcap energy and

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.598
wackiness its title suggests.

00:00:07.598 --> 00:00:10.767
Believe it or not, it only gets more bizarre
from there.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:15.248
In most cases, the first scene should properly
set the tone for the rest of the film.

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:18.770
And as you might expect, Cocaine Bear does
it with some gusto.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:24.279
We open in an appropriately whacked-out fashion
with a drug smuggler named Andrew C. Thornton

00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:27.279
kicking cocaine-filled duffel bags out of
a plane.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:31.410
Not only is he super-kicking coke-duffels
like Shawn Michaels, but he's also dancing

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:32.520
while he does it.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.180
This lets the audience know that this is not
a film that needs to be taken seriously by

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.840
immediately establishing its madcap tone.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.460
The scene concludes when Thornton clonks his
head on the door and falls to his doom.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.489
Bizarrely enough, this part of the film is
somewhat factual to real-life events, minus

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:49.730
the coke-fueled dancing ... we think.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:55.149
The real Andrew Carter Thornton II was a real-life
narcotics officer turned drug smuggler operating

00:00:55.149 --> 00:00:56.309
out of Kentucky.

00:00:56.309 --> 00:01:00.489
The film even shows archival news footage
of the actual events right before we go into

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:02.100
the fictional narrative.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.250
Much like the duffel bags of cocaine, this
scene effectively dropkicks you into the bear

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.327
bedlam to come.

00:01:07.327 --> 00:01:09.274
"A bear did cocaine!"

00:01:09.274 --> 00:01:14.049
Cocaine Bear kicks off with a scene straight
out of a Friday the 13th movie: two tourist

00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:18.700
hikers enjoying the splendor of the great
outdoors while an unseen threat lurks nearby.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:24.000
We meet Olaf and his wife Elsa enjoying their
nature walk while discussing their upcoming wedding.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.119
It's here we get a humorous bit of dialogue
where Elsa bluntly tells Olaf she doesn't

00:01:28.119 --> 00:01:30.920
want his brother's band playing at the wedding.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.000
Following this squabble, the couple spots
the titular bear emerging from the trees — much

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.689
to their immediate intrigue.

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:41.279
Olaf begins taking pictures, before both of
them realize that the bear isn't acting right

00:01:41.279 --> 00:01:44.149
— writhing around and head-butting trees.

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:48.220
Before Olaf and Elsa can even blink, the bear
begins approaching them with apparent malice

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.220
in its eyes.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:53.460
They are then attacked by the bear who proceeds
to drag Elsa away to her death right in front

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.684
of a horrified Olaf.

00:01:54.684 --> 00:01:59.728
After the bear mauls Elsa, it lets out a roar
before the film's title card drops on the screen.

00:01:59.728 --> 00:02:04.479
The title disappears, and the bear is immediately
distracted by a pretty butterfly, which she

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:05.782
chases offscreen.

00:02:05.782 --> 00:02:10.890
It's a glorious first kill that cements the
film's dark sense of humor, while also establishing

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.381
the momentum and tone for the rest of the
film.

00:02:13.778 --> 00:02:18.470
Following Cocaine Bear's first gruesome murder,
we are treated to a decade-appropriate montage

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.421
of drug public service announcements.

00:02:20.421 --> 00:02:25.790
Among these PSAs are two particularly noteworthy
ones: the famous "This is your brain on drugs"

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:29.810
commercial and an anti-cocaine PSA starring
Pee Wee Herman.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.930
These over-the-top PSAs help to immerse the
audience in the anti-drug sentiments that

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.614
were rampant in the '80s.

00:02:35.614 --> 00:02:40.810
During the decade, politicians adopted intense
anti-drug platforms, introducing things like

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:42.944
the D.A.R.E campaign and slogans like:

00:02:42.944 --> 00:02:44.004
"Just say no!"

00:02:44.004 --> 00:02:48.020
The "This is your brain on drugs" commercial
features a frying pan with some hot grease

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.430
meant to represent brain-altering substances.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:54.780
We then see an egg introduced to the pan as
a not-so-subtle metaphor for the dangers of

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:55.920
using drugs.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.000
It's definitely silly, but nothing will ever
hold a candle to Paul Reubens — in character

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.140
as Pee Wee — talking about the dangers of
crack cocaine.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.950
While definitely a noble goal, Pee Wee might
not have been the best fictional character

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:09.459
to trot out for that particular message.

00:03:09.459 --> 00:03:13.590
It's a bizarre yet hilarious little addition
and a nice dose of nostalgia for anyone who

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.880
grew up during the anti-drug messaging of
the '80s.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.769
Early on in the film, we're introduced to
Dee Dee, a young artist who wants to paint

00:03:20.769 --> 00:03:22.226
the rocks near a waterfall.

00:03:22.226 --> 00:03:25.930
Unbeknownst to her mother, Dee Dee and her
friend Henry have skipped school and ventured

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.080
off into the woods.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.220
On their wholesome little hike, the kids discover
a lone brick of cocaine in the bushes and

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.650
decide to pick it up.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:37.010
Henry begins lying that he does cocaine with
his other friend all the time, leading Dee

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:38.257
Dee to call his bluff.

00:03:38.257 --> 00:03:43.642
What ensues is a scene where these two unaccompanied
children "do coke" by eating it off a pocket knife.

00:03:43.642 --> 00:03:48.730
This, of course, does not work and both kids
immediately spit out the cocaine before continuing

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:49.909
their hike to the falls.

00:03:49.909 --> 00:03:54.040
They then discover another brick of cocaine,
but this time it appears as though someone

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.720
— or something — had already gotten into
it.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.061
This is revealed to be the bear who then makes
its presence known, terrifying both Dee Dee

00:04:01.061 --> 00:04:02.122
and Henry.

00:04:02.122 --> 00:04:06.550
As the saying goes, "don't do drugs, kids"
and these particular kids learn this lesson

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.069
in the most hilarious and frightening way.

00:04:09.069 --> 00:04:13.403
"Because drugs, especially cocaine, are very,
very bad."

00:04:13.403 --> 00:04:14.269
Bathroom knife fight

00:04:14.269 --> 00:04:17.019
One of the strangest things about Cocaine
Bear is that the bear itself doesn't commit

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:18.700
every act of violence in the movie.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:23.030
Yes, in this world, we're shown that human
beings can be just as violent and destructive

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:24.570
as drug-addled bears.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:28.770
Early on in the film, two drug dealers named
Daveed and Eddie arrive at the park in search

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.488
of their boss' lost cocaine.

00:04:30.488 --> 00:04:35.380
Upon arriving, Daveed goes to use the bathroom
where he is confronted by a trio of punk teens.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:38.919
It's mentioned just before this scene that
these teens have been causing trouble around

00:04:38.919 --> 00:04:42.479
the park — much to the chagrin of Liz the
park ranger.

00:04:42.479 --> 00:04:46.770
These teens, very foolishly, try to shake
Daveed down for his money and are subsequently

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.449
boot stomped for it.

00:04:48.449 --> 00:04:52.700
Daveed continues whooping the teens from pillar
to post, leaving them all knocked out on the

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:53.615
bathroom floor.

00:04:53.615 --> 00:04:58.590
This scene is brutal and hilarious, and a
perfect example that completely bear-free

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.169
anarchy and violence also exist in this movie.

00:05:01.169 --> 00:05:05.250
Crucially, it also establishes that these
dealers are a formidable force — setting

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.342
up the inevitable showdown between them and
the bear.

00:05:08.625 --> 00:05:12.500
Partway through the film, Dee Dee's mother
Sari ventures to the park in search of her

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:13.789
daughter and Henry.

00:05:13.789 --> 00:05:18.820
She tags along with Liz and an eccentric park
inspector named Peter to locate them.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:22.600
After venturing deeper into the woods, the
trio eventually finds Henry scared out of

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.500
his mind and halfway up a tree.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:28.660
It's not too long before the bear remerges,
horrifically wounding Liz before turning its

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.780
attention to a hysterical Peter.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.759
While fleeing from the bear, Peter crawls
for his life through some bushes before landing

00:05:34.759 --> 00:05:37.400
face-first in another pile of lost cocaine.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.210
He then takes a cue from Henry and proceeds
to climb another tree while Sari hides behind one.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:46.950
This leads to a very tense moment when Sari
just barely avoids the bear's detection and

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.319
its coke-fueled wrath.

00:05:48.319 --> 00:05:52.310
While Henry believes they will be safe above
the ground, this turns out to be a tremendous

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:55.000
mistake because bears can climb trees.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.720
Enticed by the scent of the cocaine all over
Peter, the bear rushes up the other tree and

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:01.352
begins violently eating him.

00:06:01.352 --> 00:06:05.524
Cocaine Bear's surprising stealth

00:06:05.524 --> 00:06:10.360
After bailing on Sari, Henry, and Peter, Liz
returns to the park's main building, where

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:14.389
she encounters the remaining young teens who
had encountered Daveed previously.

00:06:14.389 --> 00:06:18.920
Both of them — equally battered and bruised
— have no idea about each other's situations,

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:21.920
with Liz assuming the teens were also attacked
by the bear.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:25.560
Liz calls for some paramedics before they
notice a noise at the door which is revealed

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:26.560
to be the bear.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.070
In a moment of panic, Liz discharges her gun
and accidentally shoots one of the teens through

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:31.590
the head.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:36.010
This leaves one remaining teen who notices
along with Liz that the bear is gone, but

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:38.000
there are now noises coming from the roof.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.450
While the film wears its absurdity proudly,
it should be credited for this scene and the

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:46.349
following one as they showcase a decent amount
of suspense before belting you upside the

00:06:46.349 --> 00:06:47.823
head with a bear attack.

00:06:47.823 --> 00:06:51.830
This tension is then broken when the bear
crashes through the door's mini-window and

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.539
begins clawing the teen.

00:06:53.539 --> 00:06:57.449
The horrific scene left behind sets up the
moment a little later on when the medics arrive

00:06:57.449 --> 00:06:59.319
to investigate the carnage.

00:06:59.319 --> 00:07:03.789
A short while after the bear attacks the park's
main building, two paramedics arrive to examine

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:04.880
the crime scene.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:08.820
The paramedics — Beth and Tom — venture
inside where they find a complete bloodbath

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.160
with Liz barely clinging to life.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:15.450
Many of you might recognize comedian Scott
Seiss from his TikTok videos wherein he often

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:18.570
relatably comments on the annoying pitfalls
of retail work.

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:21.000
"You just lost yourself a customer."

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.825
"You think I own this business?"

00:07:22.825 --> 00:07:26.319
Tom checks out a backroom that appears to
have blood pooling out from under its door

00:07:26.319 --> 00:07:27.817
while Beth checks on Liz.

00:07:27.817 --> 00:07:32.290
Right on cue, the bear reveals itself to a
horrified Tom, who attempts to close the door

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:35.660
and quietly leave before the bear brings it
down on top of him.

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:39.690
Now trapped under the door, Tom attempts to
escape as Beth loads an injured Liz into the

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:40.817
waiting ambulance.

00:07:40.817 --> 00:07:45.289
Tom is able to free himself and board the
moving ambulance before the bear gives chase,

00:07:45.289 --> 00:07:47.909
easily able to keep up with the fleeing vehicle.

00:07:47.909 --> 00:07:52.129
What ensues is a ridiculous chase sequence
where Liz attempts to shoot the bear as Tom

00:07:52.129 --> 00:07:54.220
attempts to shut the door to the ambulance.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:58.160
The scene reaches its crescendo when the bear
jumps through the air and lands in the back

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.129
of the ambulance, mauling Tom and sending
Liz flying out.

00:08:02.129 --> 00:08:06.000
Beth then meets her gruesome end when she
crashes the ambulance into a tree.

00:08:06.284 --> 00:08:10.984
As the film nears its final act, the two remaining
storylines converge at the bear's cave, where

00:08:10.984 --> 00:08:12.764
Dee Dee is being held.

00:08:12.764 --> 00:08:17.460
Sari and Henry arrive, finding Dee Dee hiding
in the cave after following a trail of paint

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.710
she'd left behind for them to track.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:24.260
This coincides with Eddie and Daveed — joined
by Eddie's dad Syd, who's played by Ray Liotta

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:28.120
— also traveling to the cave to reclaim
more of the lost cocaine.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:32.860
Both parties make the same shocking discovery:
the bear has a set of cubs that are also hooked

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:33.734
on cocaine.

00:08:33.734 --> 00:08:37.640
Much like their mother, the cubs are hopped
up on a healthy blizzard of the white stuff,

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:39.455
with their fur even covered in it.

00:08:39.455 --> 00:08:43.630
Later on in the climax, Syd opts to placate
the cubs by throwing them another sizable

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:45.330
brick of cocaine.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:49.380
Definite credit to the film's marketing team
who wisely chose to keep this additional twist

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:53.070
out of the trailers as it makes for a nice
surprise for audiences.

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:57.010
It also ensures that the bear isn't just a
one-dimensional, drug-fuelled villain and

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:00.000
gives some extra stakes for the film's big
finale.

00:09:00.116 --> 00:09:04.450
Cocaine Bear reaches its epic conclusion where
all truly great films should: on the edge

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:05.181
of a cliff.

00:09:05.181 --> 00:09:09.750
Sari and the kids are now held at gunpoint
by Syd, not keen on letting any witnesses

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:10.635
get away.

00:09:10.635 --> 00:09:16.263
At the same time, Syd begins needlessly beating
on the cubs for trying to nibble at his reclaimed cocaine.

00:09:16.263 --> 00:09:20.540
His comeuppance soon arrives in the form of
the cub's mama making a grand entrance on

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:24.860
the top of the waterfall before jumping down
to the cliffside to protect her babies.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:30.200
At this time, Sari, the kids, Eddie, and Daveed
all opt to jump off the cliff and into the

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:31.701
waters below to escape.

00:09:31.701 --> 00:09:36.500
Syd is able to temporarily fight off the bear
before accidentally gifting her with one final

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:37.711
dose of cocaine.

00:09:37.711 --> 00:09:42.290
As he attempts to reclaim a bag dangling from
the falls in one final last ditch effort,

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:44.649
Syd becomes tied up in the strap of the bag.

00:09:44.649 --> 00:09:49.060
Syd then begins choking as the bear slashes
his guts out — which her cub then proceeds

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:49.849
to eat.

00:09:49.849 --> 00:09:55.130
Considering this is Ray Liotta's final onscreen
performance following his death in 2022, this

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:57.310
was one hell of a way to go out.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:02.670
It also caps the insanity of this movie nicely,
and Cocaine Bear remains bizarre and brilliant

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:04.034
right to the very end.

